# Answer 2 · Why Pakistan Ranks Second-Worst for Gender Inequality Despite Its Commitments (CSS 2018)
@py question 2018-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Explain the ranking and the commitments, then set out the reasons — sub-index by sub-index and cause by cause — for the gap between promise and practice.
@ask What REASONS are responsible for ranking Pakistan SECOND-WORST for gender inequality
@ask DESPITE international CONVENTIONS and the 1973 CONSTITUTION?
(1) Introduction: 143rd of 144 in 2017 — a gap between strong promises and weak practice
(2) The ranking explained: the Global Gender Gap Index and its four sub-indices
(3) The commitments: Constitution Articles 25 and 34; CEDAW, Beijing, SDGs
(4) Economic gap: very low female labour-force participation, pay gaps, informal work
(5) Education gap: low female literacy; girls out of school
(6) Health gap: maternal deaths, anaemia, son preference
(7) Political gap: women depend on reserved seats; few ministers
(8) Reason 1: the enforcement gap between laws and practice
(9) Reason 2: patriarchal norms — purdah, honour, early marriage
(10) Reason 3: poverty and low spending on health and education
(11) Reason 4: mobility, safety and unpaid care
(12) Reason 5: weak political will, reservations, federal and provincial gaps, crises
(13) Way forward and conclusion
:::

## Decoding the question
**"What reasons are responsible"** asks for **causes**. The question contrasts two things — the **ranking** and the **commitments** — so you must explain both and then the **gap** between them. Use the index's **four sub-indices** to show where Pakistan falls short, then give the **underlying reasons**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The ranking
In the World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Report 2017**, Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 144== countries — **second-worst** in the world, ahead only of Yemen. (By 2025 it had fallen to last, ==148th of 148==, before rising to ==143rd of 145== in ==2026==.)

#### ii) The paradox
Pakistan's **Constitution of 1973** promises equality, and Pakistan has joined major international conventions. Yet the gap between women and men remains among the widest in the world.

#### iii) The argument
The reasons lie in a persistent gap between **promise and practice**: laws are weakly enforced; patriarchal norms, poverty and low public spending limit women's health, education and work; unsafe mobility and unpaid care keep women at home; and political will and representation are weak.

### 2. The ranking explained
The **Global Gender Gap Index** (since ==2006==) measures how much of the **gap** between women and men a country has closed across four sub-indices: **economic participation and opportunity**, **educational attainment**, **health and survival**, and **political empowerment**. Pakistan scores lowest on the **economic** sub-index. Because the index measures **gaps** rather than absolute levels, a country can rank low even while making some progress, if other countries move faster — which has repeatedly happened to Pakistan.

### 3. The commitments

#### i) The Constitution
**Article 25** guarantees equality before law and forbids discrimination on the basis of sex, while allowing special provisions for women; **Article 34** requires steps to ensure women's full participation in national life; **Article 35** protects mothers; **Articles 51 and 106** reserve seats for women.

#### ii) International conventions
Pakistan acceded to **CEDAW** in ==1996==, adopted the **Beijing Platform** (==1995==), committed to the **MDGs** (==2000==) and later the **SDGs** (==2015==), and joined the core human-rights covenants.

### 4. The economic gap
Female **labour-force participation** has long been among the lowest in the world — ==24.4%== even in 2024–25, against roughly 80% for men. Most working women are in **agriculture** and **informal** work, often unpaid, and much of their labour is not counted as work at all. The ILO has reported one of the world's **widest gender pay gaps** in Pakistan, and very few women hold senior posts.

### 5. The education gap
Education is the foundation of the other dimensions, yet female literacy is far below male literacy — about ==53%== against ==68%== in the Census 2023 — and more than **25 million** children are out of school, most of them girls, especially in rural areas and Balochistan.

### 6. The health gap
Health and survival also lag. Pakistan's maternal mortality ratio was ==186== (2019); about ==42%== of women are anaemic; **son preference** affects girls' nutrition and care, and the sex ratio.

### 7. The political gap
Reserved seats have brought women into Parliament, but women's presence in the National Assembly still depends mainly on those **60 reserved seats**; few women win general seats (only ==11== in ==2024==), and women ministers are rare — in 2025 there were none.

### 8. Reason 1: the enforcement gap
This is the most important reason. Pakistan has many **pro-women laws** — on harassment (==2010==), acid attacks and anti-women practices (==2011==), honour killings (==2016==) — but police, courts and local officials often fail to apply them. Women do not know their rights; cases take years; families and *jirgas* settle crimes privately. **Laws on paper do not become rights in practice.**

### 9. Reason 2: patriarchal norms
**Purdah**, family **honour**, **son preference** and **early marriage** limit girls' schooling, women's mobility and their work outside the home. Daughters are often seen as "belonging to another household", so parents invest less in their education. The male-breadwinner ideal makes women's paid work seem unnecessary or shameful in many families.

### 10. Reason 3: poverty and low spending
Pakistan has long spent only about **1% of GDP on health** and **around 2% on education**. Weak public services hit women hardest, because families ration scarce resources in favour of sons. Rural health units often lack female staff, and many villages have no girls' secondary school.

### 11. Reason 4: mobility, safety and unpaid care

#### i) Mobility and safety
Unsafe public transport and **harassment** keep women from work and study; families often forbid daughters to travel far without an escort.

#### ii) Unpaid care
Without **childcare**, women carry the whole burden of housework and care, which limits their paid work. This unpaid work is not counted in national income, so its value is invisible to policy-makers.

::: fig cycle | Why Pakistan's gender gap persists despite its commitments.
@centre Promise without practice
Weak enforcement of laws
Patriarchal norms
Poverty and low public spending
Unsafe mobility and harassment
Unpaid care and no childcare
Weak political will and representation
Conflict, disasters and crises
:::

### 12. Reason 5: politics, federalism and crises

#### i) Political will
Gender equality is seldom a political priority, and there are few women in decision-making positions to press for it. Budgets rarely assess their effects on women, and gender units in ministries have little authority.

#### ii) Reservations and resistance
Pakistan acceded to CEDAW with a **declaration** that it applies subject to the Constitution; religious bodies such as the Council of Islamic Ideology have opposed several reforms.

#### iii) Federalism
After the **18th Amendment** (==2010==), women's development moved to the provinces, whose laws and capacity differ.

#### iv) Crises
Militancy (attacks on girls' schools in Swat and the former tribal areas), natural disasters such as the ==2022== floods, and economic crises with IMF-linked austerity have repeatedly set back women's gains.

### 13. Way forward and conclusion

#### i) Way forward
**Enforce** existing laws; invest in **girls' secondary schools** and **primary health care**; create **safe transport** and **childcare** so women can work; raise the **marriage age** to 18 in every province; appoint **women ministers** and strengthen women's representation; and track progress with **sex-disaggregated data**. The rise to 143rd in ==2026== — driven by education and political empowerment — shows that policy can move the ranking.

#### ii) Conclusion
Pakistan's low ranking is not due to a lack of promises — its Constitution and treaties are strong. It is due to a **gap between promise and practice**, rooted in norms, poverty, weak enforcement and weak political will. Closing that gap — especially in women's **economic participation** — is the key to moving up the index.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains both the **ranking** (four sub-indices) and the **commitments**, then the **gap** between them.
- Gives **five clear reasons**, each with evidence.
- A **cycle figure** shows how the reasons reinforce each other.
- Up-to-date data (2024–25, 2025, 2026) show awareness of the current position.
